          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a code must never be sequenced first, how is that shown in the classification?",
          "answer": "The retrieved passages only partly answer this, and they do not describe the exact marking used for a code that can never be listed first.\n\nWhat the passages do say, from the ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions summary (US clinical modification maintained by NCHS and CMS, educational summary): the classification uses set notes to fix how codes are sequenced. \"Code first\" and \"use additional code\" appear as a pair. They make an underlying condition get reported before its manifestation. \"Code also\" means two codes are needed, with no fixed order. Those notes are the sequencing signals in the classification.\n\nWhat the passages do not say: they do not state that a manifestation code is marked as \"cannot be principal or first-listed,\" and they do not describe any parentheses or italics convention for it. I could not confirm that from the retrieved material, so I won't state it as fact here.\n\nFor the exact rule, check the official ICD-10-CM Tabular List and its guidelines for sequencing, or ask a certified medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
